Did you mean "I don't know how to use it" or "I
don't know why to use it"?

"How:"

> Stand next to a pet and apply it to them.

> To detach, you must again stand next to the pet
> and apply the leash.

> Be careful not to pull too much, they don't like
> it and will make a noise to tell you so.

Be careful also not to use a cursed leash on your
pet, it has a nasty habit of eventually strangling
the pet as you're dragging your beloved companion
along behind you.

Nobody likes a choked pet, even your deity.

That pretty much covers "how".

As to "why", there are many situations where your
pet can save you if nearby, but you will die if your
pet has gone off exploring. A "pet on a leash" is
especially useful in the large open areas of the
gnomish mines, where monsters can approach from many
directions at once, or when first entering levels,
in case theres a grave and a crowd of monsters at
the bottom of the stairs, so you can either retreat
together away from the stairs, or run back up the
stairs together even if separated. [I'm guessing
that works, from the posted comment that the pet
doesn't have to be next to you to follow you up or
down stairs if leashed.]

Several specialized uses, like robbing shops and
credit cloning, have been mentioned by others.

It is often a good idea to keep a "not quite big
enough to take on the watch captain" pet on a leash
in Mines Town, too, to prevent losing the pet, who
tends to attack monsters just a bit too big to
handle, lots of times. You can retreat into a
closet, trust the leash soon to have the pet in the
closet doorway, then step into the doorway to
displace the pet into the closet and close the door,
much faster than doing the same sequestering without
the leash.

This is useful especially if you need to keep some
huge pet from killing a shopkeeper or a temple
priest for which you have later need.

